---
import reports from '../data/route-accuracy.json';

const duration = (seconds: number) => {
  const rounded = Math.round(seconds);
  return `${Math.floor(rounded / 3600)}h ${Math.floor(rounded % 3600 / 60)}m ${rounded % 60}s`;
};
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Accuracy | NakupniTour.cz</title>
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <style>
      body { margin: 0; background: #f5efdd; color: #302e26; font: 16px/1.65 system-ui, sans-serif; }
      main { max-width: 1080px; margin: auto; padding: 32px 20px 60px; }
      nav { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 20px; }
      a { color: #245a81; }
      a:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #245a81; outline-offset: 4px; }
      h1, h2 { font-family: Georgia, serif; }
      h1 { font-style: italic; font-size: 38px; margin-bottom: 8px; }
      .intro { max-width: 800px; }
      .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; border: 1px solid #c8ba95; border-radius: 14px; background: #faf6e9; margin: 24px 0; }
      table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14px; }
      th, td { padding: 14px; text-align: left; border-bottom: 1px solid #d9cfb4; white-space: nowrap; }
      th { color: #245a81; }
      tbody tr:last-child td { border-bottom: 0; }
      .gap { font-weight: 700; color: #245a81; }
      .note { padding: 16px 20px; border-left: 3px solid #bb3029; background: #faf6e9; }
      details { border-top: 1px solid #d9cfb4; padding: 14px 0; }
      summary { cursor: pointer; color: #245a81; }
      code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; font-size: 12px; }
      @media(max-width:600px) { h1 { font-size: 30px; } main { padding: 24px 16px; } }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <nav aria-label="Site information"><a href="/">Back to NakupniTour.cz</a><a href="/license/">License</a></nav>
      <h1>How accurate is this tour?</h1>
      <p class="intro">These are best-found road itineraries, not promises of the fastest real-world journey.
        We tested all four default, free-start routes against mathematical lower bounds using the same directed road-time matrices as the planner.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Default route optimality bounds">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Supermarket</th><th>Shops</th><th>Published driving time</th><th>Lower bound</th><th>At most above optimum</th><th>Maximum possible saving</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{reports.map(report => (
            <tr data-chain={report.slug}>
              <th scope="row">{report.name}</th><td>{report.stores}</td>
              <td>{duration(report.publishedSeconds)}</td><td>{duration(report.lowerBoundSeconds)}</td>
              <td class="gap">≤ {report.displayGapPercent.toFixed(3)}%</td>
              <td>{duration(Math.ceil(report.maximumSavingSeconds))}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Percentages are rounded <strong>up</strong> to three decimal places. Times are rounded to the nearest second;
        maximum savings are rounded up. Smaller gaps are better. A large gap means the bound is inconclusive,
        not that the route is necessarily bad.</p>
      <p class="note"><strong>The table evaluates the published itineraries.</strong> These are the routes you actually see and export.
        These bounds apply only to <strong>“Best road route”</strong>, with any starting and finishing shop and no return journey.
        The five city-start options per chain have not been independently bounded.
        {reports.every(report => !report.publishedOptimal) && ' None of the published routes has been proven absolutely optimal.'}</p>
      <h2>What does the percentage mean?</h2>
      <p>The real model optimum lies between the lower bound and a verified feasible route.
        We report <code>100 × (published time / lower bound − 1)</code>. This is an upper bound on how much slower
        the published route can be than the optimum, not a probability or a percentage of “accuracy”.</p>
      <h2>How we checked</h2>
      <p>OSRM supplies direction-dependent car-driving times between every pair of shops.
        OR-Tools tries to improve feasible routes, retaining only verified improvements across repeated searches.
        SciPy/HiGHS tightens lower bounds with directed assignment constraints and subtour-elimination cuts,
        including directed minimum-cut separation of fractional solutions in the strengthened runs.
        The incremental solver retains its LP basis and accumulated cuts, avoiding repeated cold starts.
        Odd-cut inequalities strengthen the degree-two projection. A threshold-infeasibility test can
        rule out every route faster than a chosen time, certifying a requested gap without finding the exact optimum.
        Further runs use OR-Tools CP-SAT circuit constraints with the best verified route and earlier lower bounds.
        Lin-Kernighan-Helsgaun (LKH) supplies additional directed-route candidates; it does not establish lower bounds.
        Each solver is limited to two threads.
        A zero-cost dummy stop represents the free start and finish. Per-chain budgets are listed below.
        Disconnected cycles are never accepted as a valid itinerary.</p>
      <p>All store coordinates, route orders and objective values are checked against the original matrix.
        The solver is tested against exhaustive enumeration on small directed examples, including disconnected cycle covers.
        HiGHS uses floating-point tolerances: these are numerical solver bounds, not formal exact-arithmetic certificates.
        Time limits do not guarantee an optimality proof or the same result on another computer.</p>
      <h2>Data and real-world limitations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Road snapshot: {reports[0].roadSnapshot.slice(0, 10)}. No live traffic, weather, future closures or roadworks.</li>
        <li>OpenStreetMap is not an authoritative current shop directory. Locations may be missing, duplicated, closed or incorrectly tagged. Explicitly closed mapped shops remain in the itineraries.</li>
        <li>Billa includes mapped convenience formats; Albert includes supermarkets and hypermarkets.</li>
        <li>Driving time excludes shopping, opening hours, parking, loading, driver rest and travel to the first shop.</li>
        <li>Coordinates are representative shop locations snapped to car-accessible roads, not verified entrances. Each leg is routed independently.</li>
        <li>Routing uses the Czech road extract; routes outside its coverage are not considered. The objective minimizes modeled time, not kilometres.</li>
        <li>Some addresses were recovered from unambiguous OSM objects inside shop buildings; unresolved addresses remain explicitly missing.</li>
        <li>GPX reflects the modeled route. It is not a guarantee of legal access, safe navigation or current store availability.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Per-chain evidence</h2>
      {reports.map(report => (
        <details>
          <summary>{report.name} — checked {report.checkedAt.slice(0, 10)}</summary>
          <p>Best candidate found: {duration(report.bestCandidateSeconds)}.
            Candidate improvement: {duration(report.candidateImprovementSeconds)}.
            {report.candidateImprovementSeconds > 0
              ? ' This candidate is not the published itinerary; it needs new road geometry and GPX validation before use.'
              : ' No verified unpublished candidate is faster than the current itinerary.'}</p>
          <p>Published driving time saved since the initial check: {duration(report.savedSinceInitialCheckSeconds)}.
            The under-30-minute remaining-saving target is {report.underThirtyMinutes ? 'certified by the numerical bound' : 'not yet certified'}.</p>
          <p>Lower bound: {report.lowerBoundSeconds.toFixed(1)} seconds.
            Published route: {report.publishedSeconds.toFixed(1)} seconds.
            Solver elapsed: {report.solverSeconds.toFixed(1)} seconds, excluding the heuristic phase.</p>
          <p>Latest bound run: heuristic budget {report.heuristicBudgetSeconds} seconds over {report.heuristicRuns} searches;
            bound solver budget {report.exactBudgetSeconds} seconds. Earlier verified bounds and candidates are retained.
            Method: {report.solver}.</p>
          <ul>{report.searches.map(search => (
            <li>{search.solver}: {Math.round(search.seconds)} seconds elapsed;
              best candidate {duration(search.bestSeconds)}, lower bound {duration(search.lowerSeconds)}.</li>
          ))}</ul>
          <p>Matrix SHA-256: <code>{report.matrixSha256}</code><br />
            Coordinates SHA-256: <code>{report.coordinateSha256}</code><br />
            Published order SHA-256: <code>{report.publishedOrderSha256}</code></p>
          {report.thresholdCertificate && <p>Threshold infeasibility was independently rechecked.
            Saved model SHA-256: <code>{report.certificateModelSha256}</code>.</p>}
        </details>
      ))}
      <p><a href="/license/">License and third-party data rights</a></p>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
